#{[17592186424829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424829 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is a natural number\n\nII. $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is an integer\n\nIII. $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d38c8439-616e-58d4-9fa5-c5876ae08e79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424829 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is not an integer.\n\n- $-\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-1)}{9}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-350289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424830 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424830 #edb/ref 1071 "Mina and Sora have the same number of songs in a playlist. A total of $58%$ of Mina's songs are from the 2000s, while $\\frac{13}{20}$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s. Select the options that make the statements below true.\n\n$\\,$\nA total of \n{{field-1}}\nof Sora's songs are from the 2000s. \n\n$\\,$\nTherefore, she has \n{{field-2}}\nsongs from the 2000s compared to Mina." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddb0b2b0-9c4c-5863-9ab3-ca3699a76416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424830 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to compare $\\frac{13}{20}$ with $58%.$ We can do this by writing $\\frac{13}{20}$ as a percent.\n\nFirst, we write $\\frac{13}{20}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{20}=\\frac{13\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{65}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{65}{100}=65%\n$$\n\nSince $65%>58%,$ we have the following:\n\n- A total of $65%$ of Sora's songs are from the 2000s.\n\n- Therefore, she has $\\text{more}$ songs from the 2000s compared to Mina." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424831 #edb/ref 1071 "$6\\times \\frac{3}{8}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddbc93fd-d822-55bd-bf23-39217e02eba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424831 #edb/ref 1074 "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6\\times 3}{8}=\\frac{18}{8}\n$$\n\nWe then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{8}=\\frac{18÷2}{8÷2}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{9}{4}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9÷4=2\\,\\text{R}1=2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-151066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424832 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers is prime?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8c5d328-cf41-5276-9927-daf5b992b1df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424832 #edb/ref 1074 "A prime number is a positive whole number with exactly two distinct whole number factors: itself and $1.$\n\nThe only number that satisfies this definition is $19.$ It has only two factors: $1$ and $19.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & =1\\times 19 \\\\  & =19\\times 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll the other given numbers have at least one factor distinct from itself, and $1:$\n\n- $15$ has $3$ and $5$ as factors:\n\n$$\n15=3\\times 5\n$$\n\n- $6$ has $2$ and $3$ as factors:\n\n$$\n6=2\\times 3\n$$\n\n- $8$ has $2$ and $4$ as factors:\n\n$$\n8=2\\times 4\n$$\n\n- $18$ has $2$ and $9$ as factors:\n\n$$\n18=2\\times 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $19.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424833 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4e/4e27e66c07e3271b2f03c8c275bc6deba48185e0f0f0d107f2daeece05161100.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $3\\times \\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eef69fb5-3ff0-5bf1-a2d4-52d92f4d7c61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424833 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{4}$ by $3$, we repeat the fraction model $3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52b2f58e97058deb1cbe8c01cd1ab430261c3a9f8f374a6cb650ebf0931553e9.png)\n\n \n\nThere are $3\\times 1=3$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, \n\n$$\n3\\times \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424834 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424834 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424834 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424834 #edb/ref 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $123\\,321\\,87◻$ divisible by $4?$\n\nI. $2$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $8$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424834 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef2c41de-4b26-57f5-bd27-cd0dbe211bfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424834 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $4$ if the last two digits form a number that is divisible by $4.$\n\nThe last two digits of the number $123\\,321\\,87◻$ are $7◻.$\n\n- If $2$ is substituted into the blank, then the last two digits are $72,$ which is indeed divisible by $4.$\n\n- If $4$ is substituted into the blank, then the last two digits are $74,$ which is *not* divisible by $4.$\n\n- If $8$ is substituted into the blank, then the last two digits are $78,$ which is *not* divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424835 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424835 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60613" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424835 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424835 #edb/ref 1071 "Liza can read $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ pages of a book in a minute. How many pages would she read if she read for $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ minutes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424835 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3718652-c7d4-58dc-86d1-2efd82a282a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424835 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the number of pages Liza would have read in $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ minutes, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$     \n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(3\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($10$) have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{10}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 5}{3\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{6}=4\\,\\text{R}\\,1=4\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Liza would have read $4\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ pages." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424836 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06dd2778-6877-5905-9f92-f4b181c7ce33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424836 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424837 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "09fc09aa-a7db-5dcc-96cc-70306cf4080a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424837 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424838 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15627a4c-a38f-5f6a-972c-cfb38f874232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424838 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424839 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27d317f1-eb32-5dbd-8228-05c2ce9686a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424839 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "338bc011-37c0-5e0c-8b1f-f9ec87b6fbb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424841 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b05661e-ad0e-5aa2-9cdf-5b84c8280cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424841 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424842 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a6bcdd3-808d-5a0d-b924-9044a382847b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424842 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424843 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b07e65e-6075-5d2c-960e-cdf38755c695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424843 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424844 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "580ef556-5ba2-534c-b639-0134a51bc9e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424844 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424845 